Z||000004||theogre||01-31-2000||07:15 PM||||what are you sealing with the Silicone? If you are glueing wood to wood, or wood to mettal, ((wood to concreat?))then the pannel adhesive used for building construction would be stronger. It comes in both regular and large size "caulk" tubes. Really easy to work with. permanent and water resitant/proof too. You can get the stuff the same place you get the wood. There are several kinds make sure you read the safety notes. some of them out gas pretty strong, and some are a bit flamable. <P>A small tube will lay down quite a few feet of glue line. The big tube will go on forever, or at least it will seem like it. A small tube should be good for 1 or 2 boxes depending on the size.<P>Once you open a tube tho you need to use it within a day or so. put a screw down the nozle between uses, not a nail. it pulls out the dry bit better. <P>the stuff sets for good within about an hour or so. you'll distroy the wood long before you split the joint.<P>some of it can't be used to glue plastic, it eats the plastic. Dry it won't hurt it. So it shouldn't hurt vynal stretched over a glued joint. You can't seal plex/lexan with it.<P>For maximum strength, especially with particle board and plywood, a bead of this in the joints then a few screws to pull it together will last about forever. Even if the screws do creap out, the glue won't let go. (It drys flexible.)<P>Don't get it on you. It's a pain to clean off. almost as bad as canned foam insulation.||||reg||1
Z||000005||jhogans||01-31-2000||08:32 PM||||theogre,<P>he could be using the silicon on the inside of the box.  probably running a bead where the pieces of wood meet to create a nice, airtight box.  that is what i use it for when I build speaker boxes.||||reg||1
